course: adult-life
lesson: 9
title: When money is tight
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every family has spells when money is tight: after a big bill, when work hours are cut, or when prices rise faster than pay. It does not mean the family is in danger; it means there is little left after the bills.
explain: Grown-ups then make a budget, cut treats, buy own-brand food, wait for sales and save up instead of borrowing. Some families use a food bank for a while, which is help, not shame.
explain: Hard times are also when scammers strike, with messages that look urgent and real.
explain: Example: in a kitchen in Auckland, Mum spreads the bills on the table and says "Let's sit down and do a budget." The children help write a list: rent, power, food, bus fares, and what is left. Then they choose the cheap fun: a picnic and a library trip instead of the cinema.
explain: You are not being punished when the answer is "not this month". Not asking for extras for a while helps a lot, and these spells do pass.
fact: "Money's tight" means little is left after the bills for extras; it does not mean the family is in danger.
fact: "We can't afford it" sometimes means there is no money for it, and sometimes that it is not worth the money compared with other things.
fact: Inflation means prices rise over time, so the same pound buys less; if pay rises more slowly, families are worse off.
fact: The cost of living is what it costs to pay for a home, food, energy and travel; a crisis is when these rise faster than pay.
fact: A sale lowers prices; a bargain is only a bargain if you would have bought the thing anyway.
fact: Own-brand food is often made in a similar way to famous brands but costs less because there is no big advertising.
fact: VAT is a tax on most things people buy; in UK shops it is already in the price, and children's clothes and most food have none.
fact: A budget is a plan for money: what comes in, what must go out on bills, and what is left for food, fun and saving.
fact: A food bank is a charity that gives free food parcels, usually after a referral from someone like a school or a doctor.
fact: A text saying a parcel is held and asking for a small payment is a common scam to steal bank details.
fact: Anyone offering free game coins for your login is a scammer trying to steal the account or a parent's card details.
fact: Saving up is slower than borrowing but cheaper, because no interest is paid.
exercise: Mum says "Money's a bit tight this month." What does it mean? | After the bills there is little left for extras | The family is about to lose the house and must move out | The family has no money at all | Mum has lost her purse
exercise: How can a child help when money is tight? | Not asking for extras for a while | Asking for more pocket money to help out | Hiding the bills from the grown-ups | Lending your savings to the neighbours
exercise: Dad says "We can't afford it." What might he mean? | No money for it, or not worth it compared with other things | He is angry with you and wants to punish you | The shop refuses to sell it to children | The thing is free, so there is no price
exercise: Nan says "Everything costs more these days." What is this called? | Inflation, prices rising over time | Interest, the money the bank pays you each year | A sale, prices lowered for a while | VAT, a tax on savings
exercise: On the news: "The cost of living is crazy." What is the cost of living? | What it costs to pay for a home, food, energy and travel | The price of a holiday abroad each year for the whole family | How much a family earns at work | The tax paid on a new car
exercise: Mum says "It's in the sale, half price!" When is it really a bargain? | If you would have bought it anyway | Whenever the price is cut in half | Whenever there is a red sticker | If the shop says it is a bargain
exercise: Dad says "Get the own brand, it's the same stuff." Why is own brand cheaper? | There is no big advertising to pay for | It is made from worse food that is not safe to eat | The shop gets it for free | The government pays half the price
exercise: Uncle grumbles "That's twenty percent VAT on top." What is VAT? | A tax on most things people buy | A fee the bank charges for cards every month | A discount for paying in cash | A charge for using a shopping trolley
exercise: In a UK shop, is VAT added at the till? | No, it is already included in the price | Yes, twenty percent is added at the till | Yes, but only on children's clothes and shoes | No, because nothing sold in shops is taxed
exercise: Mum says "Let's sit down and do a budget." What is a budget? | A plan of what comes in, what goes out and what is left | A list of presents for Christmas | A letter asking the bank for money | A loan to pay for the weekly shopping, paid back with interest
exercise: Mum says "We're going to the food bank this week." What is a food bank? | A charity that gives free food to people who need it | A bank that lends money to buy food, which must be paid back | A shop that sells food at full price | A place where you sell your old food
exercise: A text says "Your parcel is held, pay £1.99 here." What is it? | A scam to steal money or bank details | A real message from the post office about your parcel | A free gift from a delivery firm | A reminder from your school
exercise: What should you do with a text asking for a payment or details? | Don't click it and show a grown-up | Pay quickly because it is only £1.99 | Reply and ask them who they really are | Forward it to all your friends and family
exercise: In a game chat: "Give me your login and I'll send you free coins." What do you do? | Never share it and tell a grown-up | Share it, because the coins are free | Share only the first half of the password | Ask for the coins first, then share
exercise: Dad says "We'll save up for the holiday." Why save instead of borrowing? | It is cheaper, because no interest is paid | Saving is much faster than borrowing from a bank | Banks don't lend money for holidays | Saved money doubles every month
exercise: What helps a family save for something big? | Putting a bit aside every month and tracking it | Spending everything each month and just hoping for luck | Using a credit card for the deposit | Buying more things in the sales
test: Your little brother cries "Mum said money's tight, are we gonna be homeless?" What do you tell him? | No, it means less for extras, not danger | Yes, tight money always means losing the house | Yes, and we must pack our bags tonight | No, it means Mum has hidden lots of money under her bed
test: Your mate says "Food banks are only for lazy people." Put him right. | Any family can need one after a job loss or illness | He is right, only lazy people use them | Food banks are only for people with no house | Food banks sell food more cheaply to the rich
test: Dad, reading the news: "Prices up a lot this year, my pay up only a little." What has happened to the family? | They are worse off, as prices rose faster than pay | They are better off, because their pay went up as well | Nothing changes, because pay went up | They pay less tax because prices rose
test: Your sister says "VAT is added at the till in UK shops, so the toy's dearer than the label." Put her right. | In UK shops VAT is already in the price on the label | She is right, twenty percent always goes on at the till | VAT is only charged on toys, not food | VAT is paid by children, not grown-ups
test: Auntie bought five jumpers "cos they were practically giving them away" and never wore them. What went wrong? | A bargain is only a bargain if you needed it anyway | Jumpers are never in a sale | She should have bought ten jumpers while they were cheap | She paid full price without noticing
test: Mum, on a crackly call: "Nah, can't do the cinema, sorry, we're skint till payday." What does she mean? | She has little money left until her pay comes in | She has lost her bank card somewhere in the house | The cinema has closed down for good | She doesn't like films any more
test: A text: "HMRC: you're owed a tax refund! Click here and enter your card number." What is it most likely? | A scam to steal card details | A real refund from the tax office | A free birthday gift from the bank | A message from school about trips
test: Gran does a blind taste test: famous cornflakes vs own-brand. She can't tell. What does it show? | Own brand can be just as good and costs less | Famous brands are always better, whatever the price | Own-brand food is unsafe to eat | Gran's taste buds have stopped working
test: Your brother asks for new trainers; Dad says "can't afford it right now, maybe save towards it?" What's a good response? | Make a plan to save some pocket money towards them | Ask Nan to buy them on a credit card | Sulk until Dad gives in | Buy them now on buy now pay later
test: Overheard: "Everyone's struggling, it's not just us, the whole street's feeling it." What is going on? | A cost of living crisis affects many families at once | Only one family has money problems and everyone knows | The street has lost its electricity | The neighbours have all won the lottery
test: You write: pocket money £5, save £2, spend £3. What have you made? | A budget | A mortgage | A subscription | A credit score
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises inflation? | A general rise in prices, whereby a given sum purchases less | A general fall in prices, whereby a given sum purchases more | A tax levied upon the purchase of goods | Interest paid upon sums held on deposit
exam: Identify the course of action consistent with prudence upon receipt of an unsolicited message demanding a payment for the release of a parcel. | Not to follow the link, and to inform a responsible adult | To remit the sum requested forthwith, it being trivial in amount | To reply, supplying the card number requested | To forward the message to all known contacts
exam: Select the statement consistent with the established facts concerning food banks. | Charities providing food to households in need, frequently upon referral | They are financial institutions which lend money for the purchase of groceries | They sell foodstuffs at full price to all persons | They are open exclusively to persons without a dwelling
exam: Ascertain the definition of a household budget. | A plan of income, necessary expenditure and the remainder | A loan obtained for the purchase of provisions | A tax payable upon household goods | A record of interest accrued upon savings
